WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
(upbeat music)

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.090
- [Narrator] This is a story about a farm.

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:04.099
Not just any farm,

00:00:04.099 --> 00:00:08.010
but a place where imagination
sprouts up the soil.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:12.070
- We grow really kooky, weird,
wild, wonderful, tasty stuff.

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:14.350
Honestly, the weirder the better.

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:15.830
- Like what?
- Mashua,

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:16.663
oca,

00:00:16.663 --> 00:00:17.690
Badger flame beet.

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:18.680
squashini,

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:19.600
Butterfly sorrel,

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:20.490
Sunset sorrel,

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:21.520
pichuberries,

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:22.360
pineberries,

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:23.700
white alpine strawberries,

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:26.280
ice plant.
- Wait, what was the last one?

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:27.500
- What you never heard of ice plant?

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.056
- [Narrator] Uh, no.

00:00:29.056 --> 00:00:31.639
(upbeat music)

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.410
- [Narrator] Meet Aaron Choi.

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:37.950
Aaron never had any intentions

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:39.360
of getting into farming.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.341
- I had actually planned
to grab a Master's

00:00:42.341 --> 00:00:45.694
and then my PhD in
order to teach the study

00:00:45.694 --> 00:00:47.511
of religion in college.

00:00:47.511 --> 00:00:51.139
I took a complete 180.

00:00:51.139 --> 00:00:54.760
- [Narrator] That 180 is
thanks to his parents.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:56.750
After running a successful flower shop,

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:59.047
Aaron and his sisters tried
to get them to retire.

00:00:59.047 --> 00:01:01.219
But his parents had other plans.

00:01:01.219 --> 00:01:04.343
- Their retirement was
to go buy a farm instead.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:09.478
- They started off
growing Korean vegetables

00:01:09.478 --> 00:01:11.220
for retail markets.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:12.724
But then Aaron got involved,

00:01:12.724 --> 00:01:15.266
and things took a turn for the bizarre.

00:01:15.266 --> 00:01:17.050
- After I got started getting

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:18.760
my hands dirty pulling weeds,

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.900
obsessing over growing conditions,

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:22.897
light, water, temperature,

00:01:22.897 --> 00:01:25.028
humidity everything around it,

00:01:25.028 --> 00:01:28.057
it just became one elevated experiment

00:01:28.057 --> 00:01:31.078
after another; and I think
that's where I was hooked.

00:01:31.078 --> 00:01:35.479
And all of that rang and
resonated far better with me

00:01:35.479 --> 00:01:38.718
than sticking my nose
in books all the time,

00:01:38.718 --> 00:01:39.837
quite frankly.

00:01:39.837 --> 00:01:41.900
- [Narrator] Once he familiarized himself

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:44.120
with farm life he took the R&amp;D

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:45.380
to another level.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:46.560
- We started experimenting with

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.040
a lot of different greens.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.520
Then, that very quickly
blew off into a whole host

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:54.930
of other relatively unfamiliar products

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:56.090
like Pichu Berries,

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:57.650
Sweet Jade green tomatoes,

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.150
which nobody's heard of,

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:00.900
and my favorites,

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.920
oka and Mashua, which even fewer people

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:06.273
have heard of let alone actually tasted.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.520
The goal has always been to target, first

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:16.300
and foremost, high-end restaurants

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:17.400
because I think that there's a lot

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.764
of trickle down effect on
new and strange ingredients.

00:02:20.764 --> 00:02:22.260
- [Narrator] So how does one pivot

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.120
from selling bulk retail items

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.870
to supplying fancy high-end restaurants?

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:29.650
- I literally started making cold visits.

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:32.372
Once the chefs started
seeing what we were growing,

00:02:32.372 --> 00:02:35.426
to my surprise they were blown
away by the quality of it.

00:02:35.426 --> 00:02:37.270
I regularly get comments

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:39.453
like, I can't believe you're growing this.

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:41.505
- Good?
- Yeah.

00:02:41.505 --> 00:02:42.745
- Tart?
- Sweet.

00:02:42.745 --> 00:02:43.578
- Good.

00:02:43.578 --> 00:02:45.930
It's the specialty side

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:48.380
where my obsession fits in line

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:51.396
with trying to familiarize the unfamiliar.

00:02:51.396 --> 00:02:54.270
There's a lot to be said about

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:56.070
these unfamiliar foods of the world

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:58.469
that we really need to
introduce more people to.

00:02:58.469 --> 00:03:02.056
Because the more we popularize,

00:03:02.056 --> 00:03:04.130
the more conversations happen

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:05.130
around those foods;

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:08.053
without food we don't have conversation.

